@page "/"
@layout MainLayout

<PageTitle>Bortable - drag-and-drop sortable lists for Blazor</PageTitle>

@* The landing page: hero → proof strip → feature grid → code walkthrough → comparison with a
   JavaScript wrapper → persona tiles → FAQ → final call to action. Everything else on the site is
   a documentation page under DocsLayout. The hero's sample is a live list rather than a picture of
   one - a drag-and-drop library is best introduced by being dragged. *@

<div class="bb-landing">

    @* ============================== Hero ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-hero">
        <div class="bb-hero-copy">
            <span class="bb-hero-eyebrow">
                <span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span>
                Open source · MIT · net10.0 / net11.0
            </span>
            <h1>Sortable lists for Blazor,<br /><span class="bb-hero-accent">dragged in C#.</span></h1>
            <p class="bb-hero-sub">
                Reorder items, drag them between lists, clone, swap and multi-drag - with the sorting
                engine written in C# on the browser's own drag-and-drop events. Not a wrapper around
                SortableJS: your <code>IList&lt;TItem&gt;</code> is changed in place, so the order a user
                makes is the order your code reads back.
            </p>
            <pre class="bb-code bb-hero-install">dotnet add package Bit.Bortable</pre>
            <div class="bb-hero-actions">
                <a href="getting-started" class="btn btn-primary bb-btn-lg">Get started →</a>
                <a href="simple-list" class="btn btn-secondary bb-btn-lg">Live demos</a>
                <a class="btn btn-secondary bb-btn-lg" href="@SiteInfo.ProjectUrl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub ↗</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        @* The sample, framed as a window: the markup on top, and the list it renders underneath -
           live, so the first thing a visitor can do here is drag something. *@
        <div class="bb-hero-code">
            <div class="bb-browser">
                <div class="bb-browser-bar">
                    <span class="bb-browser-dots" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
                    <span class="bb-browser-url">Backlog.razor</span>
                </div>
                <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">Bortable</span> @@bind-Items=<span class="s">"_backlog"</span> TItem=<span class="s">"string"</span> Group=<span class="s">"board"</span>&gt;
    &lt;<span class="k">ItemTemplate</span> Context=<span class="s">"task"</span>&gt;@@task&lt;/<span class="k">ItemTemplate</span>&gt;
&lt;/<span class="k">Bortable</span>&gt;</pre>
                <div class="home-hero-live">
                    <Bortable @bind-Items="_hero" TItem="string" Id="hero-list"
                              Class="demo-list" ItemClass="demo-item" GhostClass="demo-ghost">
                        <ItemTemplate Context="task">@task</ItemTemplate>
                    </Bortable>
                    <p class="demo-order"><strong>_backlog:</strong> <span id="hero-order">@string.Join(", ", _hero)</span></p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Proof strip ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-proof">
        <div><strong>Engine in C#</strong><span>where it lands, what moves, what is raised</span></div>
        <div><strong>Your own list</strong><span><code>@@bind-Items</code> changes it in place</span></div>
        <div><strong>SortableJS feature set</strong><span>groups, clone, swap, multi-drag, thresholds</span></div>
        <div><strong>Every hosting model</strong><span>WebAssembly, Server, Web App and Hybrid</span></div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Feature grid ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">Capabilities</p>
        <h2>Everything SortableJS does - in Blazor</h2>
        <p class="bb-section-sub">
            The SortableJS feature set and its options, ported to a Blazor component: the same names,
            the same thresholds, the same plugins - with a strongly typed <code>Bortable&lt;TItem&gt;</code>
            in front of them.
        </p>
        <div class="bb-grid">
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Live reordering</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Items slide out of the way while you drag, with FLIP animations - and the bound list
                    is reordered in place when you drop.
                </p>
                <a href="simple-list" class="btn btn-outline">Simple list →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Shared lists</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Lists with the same <code>Group</code> exchange items, with a ghost preview of where
                    the item will land - and pull and put rules per list.
                </p>
                <a href="shared" class="btn btn-outline">Shared lists →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Clone, swap and multi-drag</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Copy an item out of a palette, drop one item onto another to trade places, or select
                    several and drag them as one block - the SortableJS plugins, built in.
                </p>
                <a href="multi-drag" class="btn btn-outline">Multi-drag →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-danger"></span> Handles and filters</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Limit dragging to a grip so the rest of the row stays clickable, and lock items with a
                    predicate so they cannot be picked up.
                </p>
                <a href="handle" class="btn btn-outline">Handle →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Swap thresholds</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    <code>SwapThreshold</code>, <code>InvertSwap</code> and <code>Direction</code> ported from
                    SortableJS, including its guard against items of different sizes swapping back and forth.
                </p>
                <a href="thresholds" class="btn btn-outline">Thresholds →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Any layout</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Vertical lists, horizontal strips, CSS grids, right-to-left rows and nested trees whose
                    items move between branches and depths.
                </p>
                <a href="nested" class="btn btn-outline">Nested sortables →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Keyboard and screen readers</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Pick an item up with Space, move it with the arrows - across lists too - and drop it; every
                    step is announced, in words you can translate, and buttons can move items without a drag.
                </p>
                <a href="accessibility" class="btn btn-outline">Keyboard →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-danger"></span> Rules and rollback</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Refuse a position as it is reached (<code>CanDrop</code>), cap a list (<code>MaxItems</code>),
                    and let a server have the last word on a drop (<code>ConfirmDrop</code>) - refused, it slides back.
                </p>
                <a href="rules" class="btn btn-outline">Rules →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Touch, pen and mouse</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    A finger or a pen drags with pointer events - a long press picks an item up, a swipe still
                    scrolls - and a drag near an edge scrolls the list or the page.
                </p>
                <a href="touch" class="btn btn-outline">Touch and pen →</a>
            </section>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Code walkthrough ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">In practice</p>
        <h2>See it in code</h2>
        <div class="bb-walkthrough">
            <div class="bb-walkthrough-row">
                <div class="bb-walkthrough-copy">
                    <span class="bb-step">01</span>
                    <h3>A kanban board is two lists and a group</h3>
                    <p>
                        Give two lists the same <code>Group</code> and their items move between them. Each
                        list stays your own <code>IList</code>, so the board's state is just two fields.
                    </p>
                    <a href="shared">Shared lists →</a>
                </div>
                <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">Bortable</span> @@bind-Items=<span class="s">"_todo"</span> TItem=<span class="s">"Card"</span> Group=<span class="s">"board"</span>&gt;…&lt;/<span class="k">Bortable</span>&gt;
&lt;<span class="k">Bortable</span> @@bind-Items=<span class="s">"_done"</span> TItem=<span class="s">"Card"</span> Group=<span class="s">"board"</span>&gt;…&lt;/<span class="k">Bortable</span>&gt;</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="bb-walkthrough-row">
                <div class="bb-walkthrough-copy">
                    <span class="bb-step">02</span>
                    <h3>A palette clones, a target accepts</h3>
                    <p>
                        <code>Pull="BortablePull.Clone"</code> copies items out and keeps the originals;
                        <code>Sort="false"</code> and <code>Put="false"</code> make the palette read-only.
                    </p>
                    <a href="cloning">Cloning →</a>
                </div>
                <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">Bortable</span> Items=<span class="s">"_widgets"</span> TItem=<span class="s">"Widget"</span> Group=<span class="s">"form"</span>
          Pull=<span class="s">"BortablePull.Clone"</span> Sort=<span class="s">"false"</span> Put=<span class="s">"false"</span>&gt;…&lt;/<span class="k">Bortable</span>&gt;</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="bb-walkthrough-row">
                <div class="bb-walkthrough-copy">
                    <span class="bb-step">03</span>
                    <h3>React to the drop</h3>
                    <p>
                        The SortableJS lifecycle - <code>OnStart</code>, <code>OnAdd</code>,
                        <code>OnRemove</code>, <code>OnUpdate</code>, <code>OnEnd</code> - is raised in C#,
                        with the item, the lists and both indexes in its arguments.
                    </p>
                    <a href="events">Events →</a>
                </div>
                <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">Bortable</span> @@bind-Items=<span class="s">"_done"</span> TItem=<span class="s">"Card"</span> OnAdd=<span class="s">"Completed"</span>&gt;…&lt;/<span class="k">Bortable</span>&gt;

<span class="k">private</span> Task Completed(BortableEventArgs&lt;Card&gt; e)
    =&gt; api.MarkDoneAsync(e.Item.Id);   <span class="c">// e.FromId, e.OldIndex, e.NewIndex …</span></pre>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Comparison ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">Comparison</p>
        <h2>Why not wrap SortableJS?</h2>
        <p class="bb-section-sub">
            A JavaScript wrapper moves DOM nodes behind Blazor's back and then has to reconcile the
            render tree with what it did. Bortable keeps the decision in C#, so there is nothing to
            reconcile.
        </p>
        <div class="bb-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="A SortableJS wrapper compared with Bortable">
            <table class="bb-table bb-compare">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Concern</th><th>SortableJS wrapper</th><th>Bortable</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Who moves the items</td><td>JavaScript, in the DOM</td><td>C#, in your list - Blazor renders the result</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Bound data</td><td>Patched after the fact from DOM indexes</td><td><code>@@bind-Items</code>, changed in place</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Typed items</td><td>Indexes and element ids</td><td><code>Bortable&lt;TItem&gt;</code> and typed event arguments</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Script to ship</td><td>The library plus interop glue</td><td>A small module, imported on demand</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Hosting models</td><td>Usually WebAssembly-first</td><td>WebAssembly, Server, Web App and Hybrid</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Persona CTAs ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">Where to start</p>
        <h2>Choose your path</h2>
        <div class="bb-grid">
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> New to Bortable?</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">Install, register, link the stylesheet - a sortable list in five minutes.</p>
                <a href="getting-started" class="btn btn-primary">Getting started →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Want to see it live?</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">Every feature has a page you can drag around, with its own source one click away.</p>
                <a href="simple-list" class="btn btn-outline">Open the demos →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Building with an AI agent?</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">This site is an MCP server: point your agent at <code>/mcp</code> and it reads the shipped library instead of guessing.</p>
                <a href="mcp-server" class="btn btn-outline">Try the MCP server →</a>
