    @* ============================== FAQ ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">Questions</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked</h2>
        <div class="bb-faq">
            <details>
                <summary>Is this SortableJS running through JavaScript interop?</summary>
                <p>
                    No. The sorting engine - where the item goes, which list may take it, what is moved,
                    cloned or swapped, and which events are raised - is C#. A small module does what has to
                    happen where the pointer is: it follows the drag, measures the pointer against the items
                    for the swap thresholds, plays the FLIP animations, and calls .NET only when the drop
                    position changes - so a drag stays smooth on Blazor Server too.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Which render modes does it work in?</summary>
                <p>
                    Any interactive one: WebAssembly, Server, Auto and Hybrid, on .NET 10 and 11. Pages are
                    prerendered as usual; the lists become draggable once the page is interactive.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Can the items be anything, not just strings?</summary>
                <p>
                    Yes - <code>Bortable&lt;TItem&gt;</code> is generic, and <code>ItemTemplate</code> renders
                    whatever you like inside each item. Items are rendered with <code>@@key</code>, so they must
                    be unique within a list.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Does it work on touch screens?</summary>
                <p>
                    Yes. A mouse drags with the browser's own HTML5 drag and drop; a finger or a pen drags with
                    pointer events, so touch works in every mobile browser and web view, whether or not it
                    supports native drag. A long press picks an item up, so a swipe over the list still scrolls
                    the page.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Can it be used without a mouse?</summary>
                <p>
                    Yes. Every list can be sorted from the keyboard - Space picks an item up, the arrows move it,
                    Space drops it, Escape puts it back - and each step is announced to screen readers.
                    <code>MoveAsync</code> and <code>MoveToAsync</code> move an item from code, for "move up"
                    buttons and item menus.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Can my code refuse a drop?</summary>
                <p>
                    Yes: <code>CanDrop</code> vets every position before it is shown, <code>MaxItems</code> caps a
                    list, and <code>ConfirmDrop</code> asks your code - a server, a dialog - once the item is
                    dropped, putting everything back if it says no.
                </p>
            </details>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Final CTA ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-final-cta">
        <h2>Put your lists in order</h2>
        <p class="bb-section-sub">One package, one service, one stylesheet - and any list becomes sortable.</p>
        <pre class="bb-code bb-hero-install">dotnet add package Bit.Bortable</pre>
        <div class="bb-hero-actions">
            <a href="getting-started" class="btn btn-primary bb-btn-lg">Get started →</a>
            <a href="simple-list" class="btn btn-secondary bb-btn-lg">Browse the demos</a>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

